he white blood cells are missing the substance needed to kill bacteria. This may be due to chronic granulomatous disease. See full list on medlineplus.gov There is little risk involved with having your blood taken. Veins and arteries vary in size from one person to another and from one side of the body to the other. Taking blood from some people may be more difficult than from others. Other risks associated with having blood drawn are slight, but may include: •Excessive bleeding •Fainting or feeling lightheaded •Multiple punctures to locate veins •Hematoma (blood accumulating under the skin) See full list on medlineplus.gov Aug 25, 2026 · Workup: Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) is a primary immunodeficiency that affects phagocytes of the innate immune system and leads to recurrent or persistent intracellular bacterial and... The Nitroblue Tetrazolium test is an essential tool for diagnosing and managing immune system disorders, particularly chronic granulomatous disease (CGD). CGD is a genetic condition that impairs neutrophils’ ability to produce the oxidative burst required to eliminate bacteria and fungi. These may include severe or recurrent bacterial or fungal infections, or enlarged lymph nodes. Most people will have symptoms by the time they are three years old. In patients with CGD, the NADPH oxidase system fails to generate Superoxide and related oxygen intermediates with resultant susceptibility to recurrent bacterial and fungal infections. Jan 11, 2023 · Due to the lack of widespread availability of flow cytometry services for immunodeficiency, nitroblue tetrazolium test (NBT) is the commonly used screening modality to identify patients with chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) in developing countries."}, {"title": "Nitroblue Tetrazolium Test: Key to Diagnosing Immune ...", "url": "https://thekingsleyclinic.com/resources/nitroblue-tetrazolium-test-key-to-diagnosing-immune-disorders/", "snippet": "The Nitroblue Tetrazolium test is an essential tool for 